{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787994874362,"sessionID":"ses_fb3337e5bffeqUmulSYU99zDKc","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_b87f068bc2124557996db3e5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/brother_api-routes-for-settings/implementation.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/brother_api-routes-for-settings/implementation.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Implementation: api routes for settings\n2: \n3: id: brother\n4: status: open\n5: developer:\n6: date: 2026-08-29\n7: \n8: <!-- Produced by @developer after implementation. -->\n9: \n10: ## Summary\n11: \n12: The brief asked for settings routes on `mg serve` so a web UI can control\n13: mg: the default profile (global), and the base branch + prefix per project.\n14: The prior session that scoped this job decided to fold the @analyst breakdown\n15: into the developer pass (tasks.md), so the tasks below were drafted together\n16: with the implementation.\n17: \n18: TASK-1 (settings.go): GET/PUT `/settings` — the global default profile.\n19: Storage and write path are exactly `mg profiles <name>`'s: `MANIGOT_PROFILE`\n20: in manigot's `.env`, written via `config.UpsertEnv`, so CLI, TUI, and this\n21: API share one default and a switch made over HTTP changes what the next bare\n22: `mg`, TUI, and mg-jdi launch use. GET reports the effective default\n23: (`config.EnvValue`, falling back to `claude-pro` — the same \"Active default\"\n24: chain `mg profiles` displays); PUT validates the id against\n25: `config.ProfileByID` (unknown → 400) and persists. The envelope carries only\n26: a plain profile id, never credential material (readiness stays in `/health`).\n27: \n28: TASK-2 (settings.go): GET/PUT `/projects/{project}/settings` — the project's\n29: `baseBranch` + `jobBranchPrefix`. Storage and writer are the TUI settings\n30: screen's: `.manigot/manigot.json` in the target project, read via\n31: `project.Load` and written via `project.Save` — the sanctioned host-side\n32: writer for the one directory outside `docs/` manigot tooling owns. The PUT is\n33: a wholesale replacement (absent field clears to default: baseBranch → `main`,\n34: prefix → none), validated as ref components (`refComponentProblem` — no\n35: spaces/control chars, `..`, leading `-`, `.lock`, etc.) so garbage is a 400\n36: at write time instead of an opaque git failure later, and takes the project\n37: lock so the write serializes with create/done, which read these settings\n38: inside their own locked sections.\n39: \n40: TASK-3 (settings_test.go + surface pins): a dedicated settings test suite\n41: covering the default chain, the shared-write guarantee (existing .env lines\n42: survive), validation 400s, the project settings roundtrip through the API and\n43: `project.Load`, the fact that a rejected PUT never writes the settings file,\n44: and that the settings written actually drive job creation (a `jobBranchPrefix`\n45: of `mg` makes the next API-created job branch under `mg/`). The credentials/\n46: leak suite and the hostile-segment suite enumerate the new surface exactly as\n47: they enumerate the existing one: PUTs' envelopes carry only profile ids and\n48: ref names and their 400s never echo `.env` content; both the project GET and\n49: PUT go through the same `resolveProject` choke point.\n50: \n51: TASK-4 (docs/AGENTS.md): the listener section now documents the four routes,\n52: their validation, the lock-taking set (project settings PUT takes the\n53: project lock; the global settings write does not — no project scope), and the\n54: zero-path-inputs/credentials invariants they inherit.\n55: \n56: ## Changes\n57: \n58: - `src/internal/serve/settings.go` (new) — the four handlers, the request/\n59:   response shapes, `activeProfile`, and `refComponentProblem` ref-component\n60:   validation.\n61: - `src/internal/serve/server.go` — route registration for GET/PUT `/settings`\n62:   and GET/PUT `/projects/{project}/settings`.\n63: - `src/internal/serve/settings_test.go` (new) — the settings test suite\n64:   (defaults, shared write, roundtrip, validation, drives-job-creation,\n65:   ref-shape unit checks).\n66: - `src/internal/serve/credentials_test.go` — the credentials-never-returned\n67:   surface now enumerates the four settings routes (including the 400 paths).\n68: - `src/internal/serve/security_test.go` — the hostile-segment suite now covers\n69:   the project segment on both settings routes.\n70: - `docs/AGENTS.md` — listener/control-plane section documents the settings\n71:   endpoints.\n72: \n73: ## Verification\n74: \n75: - `go build ./...` passes.\n76: - `go vet ./...` passes.\n77: - `go test ./internal/serve/ ./internal/config/ ./internal/project/` passes\n78:   (the packages this change touches and their dependencies).\n79: - The full suite's failures are confined to packages whose tests shell out to\n80:   `git init`/`git worktree` (`cmd/mg`, `internal/git`, `internal/job`,\n81:   `internal/session`, `internal/ui`) — every one is the agent container's git\n82:   shim refusing those commands, an environmental condition unrelated to this\n83:   change (confirmed: 284 shim refusals, zero non-shim failure causes). The\n84:   change touches none of those packages.\n85: \n86: ## Known issues / follow-ups\n87: \n88: - None in scope. The settings API is deliberately limited to the brief's\n89:   three settings (profile, baseBranch, jobBranchPrefix); the OpenCode theme\n90:   and other `.env` values stay out of the HTTP surface, consistent with the\n91:   \"credentials never returned\" invariant.\n\n(End of file - total 91 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Implementation: api routes for settings\n\nid: brother\nstatus: open\ndeveloper:\ndate: 2026-08-29\n\n<!-- Produced by @developer after implementation. -->\n\n## Summary\n\nThe brief asked for settings routes on `mg serve` so a web UI can control\nmg: the default profile (global), and the base branch + prefix per project.\nThe prior session that scoped this job decided to fold the @analyst breakdown\ninto the developer pass (tasks.md), so the tasks below were drafted together\nwith the implementation.\n\nTASK-1 (settings.go): GET/PUT `/settings` — the global default profile.\nStorage and write path are exactly `mg profiles <name>`'s: `MANIGOT_PROFILE`\nin manigot's `.env`, written via `config.UpsertEnv`, so CLI, TUI, and this","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/brother_api-routes-for-settings/implementation.md","text":"# Implementation: api routes for settings\n\nid: brother\nstatus: open\ndeveloper:\ndate: 2026-08-29\n\n<!-- Produced by @developer after implementation. -->\n\n## Summary\n\nThe brief asked for settings routes on `mg serve` so a web UI can control\nmg: the default profile (global), and the base branch + prefix per project.\nThe prior session that scoped this job decided to fold the @analyst breakdown\ninto the developer pass (tasks.md), so the tasks below were drafted together\nwith the implementation.\n\nTASK-1 (settings.go): GET/PUT `/settings` — the global default profile.\nStorage and write path are exactly `mg profiles <name>`'s: `MANIGOT_PROFILE`\nin manigot's `.env`, written via `config.UpsertEnv`, so CLI, TUI, and this\nAPI share one default and a switch made over HTTP changes what the next bare\n`mg`, TUI, and mg-jdi launch use. GET reports the effective default\n(`config.EnvValue`, falling back to `claude-pro` — the same \"Active default\"\nchain `mg profiles` displays); PUT validates the id against\n`config.ProfileByID` (unknown → 400) and persists. The envelope carries only\na plain profile id, never credential material (readiness stays in `/health`).\n\nTASK-2 (settings.go): GET/PUT `/projects/{project}/settings` — the project's\n`baseBranch` + `jobBranchPrefix`. Storage and writer are the TUI settings\nscreen's: `.manigot/manigot.json` in the target project, read via\n`project.Load` and written via `project.Save` — the sanctioned host-side\nwriter for the one directory outside `docs/` manigot tooling owns. The PUT is\na wholesale replacement (absent field clears to default: baseBranch → `main`,\nprefix → none), validated as ref components (`refComponentProblem` — no\nspaces/control chars, `..`, leading `-`, `.lock`, etc.) so garbage is a 400\nat write time instead of an opaque git failure later, and takes the project\nlock so the write serializes with create/done, which read these settings\ninside their own locked sections.\n\nTASK-3 (settings_test.go + surface pins): a dedicated settings test suite\ncovering the default chain, the shared-write guarantee (existing .env lines\nsurvive), validation 400s, the project settings roundtrip through the API and\n`project.Load`, the fact that a rejected PUT never writes the settings file,\nand that the settings written actually drive job creation (a `jobBranchPrefix`\nof `mg` makes the next API-created job branch under `mg/`). The credentials/\nleak suite and the hostile-segment suite enumerate the new surface exactly as\nthey enumerate the existing one: PUTs' envelopes carry only profile ids and\nref names and their 400s never echo `.env` content; both the project GET and\nPUT go through the same `resolveProject` choke point.\n\nTASK-4 (docs/AGENTS.md): the listener section now documents the four routes,\ntheir validation, the lock-taking set (project settings PUT takes the\nproject lock; the global settings write does not — no project scope), and the\nzero-path-inputs/credentials invariants they inherit.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `src/internal/serve/settings.go` (new) — the four handlers, the request/\n  response shapes, `activeProfile`, and `refComponentProblem` ref-component\n  validation.\n- `src/internal/serve/server.go` — route registration for GET/PUT `/settings`\n  and GET/PUT `/projects/{project}/settings`.\n- `src/internal/serve/settings_test.go` (new) — the settings test suite\n  (defaults, shared write, roundtrip, validation, drives-job-creation,\n  ref-shape unit checks).\n- `src/internal/serve/credentials_test.go` — the credentials-never-returned\n  surface now enumerates the four settings routes (including the 400 paths).\n- `src/internal/serve/security_test.go` — the hostile-segment suite now covers\n  the project segment on both settings routes.\n- `docs/AGENTS.md` — listener/control-plane section documents the settings\n  endpoints.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `go build ./...` passes.\n- `go vet ./...` passes.\n- `go test ./internal/serve/ ./internal/config/ ./internal/project/` passes\n  (the packages this change touches and their dependencies).\n- The full suite's failures are confined to packages whose tests shell out to\n  `git init`/`git worktree` (`cmd/mg`, `internal/git`, `internal/job`,\n  `internal/session`, `internal/ui`) — every one is the agent container's git\n  shim refusing those commands, an environmental condition unrelated to this\n  change (confirmed: 284 shim refusals, zero non-shim failure causes). The\n  change touches none of those packages.\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\n- None in scope. The settings API is deliberately limited to the brief's\n  three settings (profile, baseBranch, jobBranchPrefix); the OpenCode theme\n  and other `.env` values stay out of the HTTP surface, consistent with the\n  \"credentials never returned\" invariant.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":91,"totalLines":91,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/brother_api-routes-for-settings/implementation.md","time":{"start":1787994874352,"end":1787994874359}},"id":"prt_04ccca16b001chijuCEuJOLWgC","sessionID":"ses_fb3337e5bffeqUmulSYU99zDKc","messageID":"msg_04ccc9ddd001cLcmrjwmZlYcks"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787994874899,"sessionID":"ses_fb3337e5bffeqUmulSYU99zDKc","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_19b769623439495b95ebae1e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/brother_api-routes-for-settings/tasks.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/brother_api-routes-for-settings/tasks.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Tasks: api routes for settings\n2: \n3: id: brother\n4: status: open\n5: analyst: developer session (breakdown drafted together with implementation — the job ran without a separate @analyst pass)\n6: date: 2026-08-29\n7: \n8: <!-- Produced from brief.md. -->\n9: \n10: ## Task breakdown\n11: \n12: <!-- TASK-1: description\n13:      files: list of files likely affected\n14:      depends: none\n15:      risk: low / medium / high — reason\n16: -->\n17: \n18: TASK-1: Settings API routes — global default profile\n19: files: src/internal/serve/settings.go (new), src/internal/serve/server.go\n20: depends: none\n21: risk: low — additive routes; the write path reuses config.UpsertEnv, the\n22: exact primitive `mg profiles <name>` writes MANIGOT_PROFILE with, so CLI,\n23: TUI, and API keep sharing one default. The profile id is validated against\n24: config.ProfileByID, so an unknown id is a 400, and only a plain profile id\n25: (ever a non-secret) is read back.\n26: \n27: TASK-2: Settings API routes — per-project baseBranch + jobBranchPrefix\n28: files: src/internal/serve/settings.go (new), src/internal/serve/server.go\n29: depends: TASK-1 (same file, shared helpers)\n30: risk: low — additive routes; writes go through project.Save (the sanctioned\n31: writer the TUI already uses) into the target project's .manigot/manigot.json.\n32: Ref-component validation rejects garbage before it can reach later git argv;\n33: the write takes the project lock so it serializes with create/done, which\n34: read these settings inside their own locked sections.\n35: \n36: TASK-3: Tests + surface pi